      <li>U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/compliance-officers.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Compliance Officers</a>, 2025 (3% growth; $78,420 median).</li>
      <li>U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, <a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/network-and-computer-systems-administrators.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Network and Computer Systems Administrators</a>, 2025 (projected to decline 4%).</li>
      <li>McKinsey Global Institute, <a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/a-new-future-of-work-the-race-to-deploy-ai-and-raise-skills-in-europe-and-beyond" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A New Future of Work</a>, 2024 (about 30% of US hours automatable by 2030).</li>
      <li>McKinsey Global Institute, <a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/agents-robots-and-us-skill-partnerships-in-the-age-of-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agents, Robots, and Us</a>, 2025 (human oversight, validation, and exception handling remain vital).</li>
      <li>World Economic Forum, <a href="https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-future-of-jobs-report-2025/in-full/3-skills-outlook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Future of Jobs Report 2025, Skills Outlook</a> (dependability now treated as table stakes).</li>
      <li>Harvard Business Review, <a href="https://hbr.org/2026/02/when-being-the-most-reliable-leader-becomes-a-liability" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">When Being the Most Reliable Leader Becomes a Liability</a>, 2026 (trust to dependence to single point of failure).</li>
      <li>Harvard Business Review, <a href="https://hbr.org/2018/06/how-are-you-protecting-your-high-performers-from-burnout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How Are You Protecting Your High Performers from Burnout?</a>, 2018.</li>
      <li>Kaplan, R. and Kaiser, R., <a href="http://fearyourstrengths.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fear Your Strengths</a>, 2013 (overusing a strength is underperformance).</li>
